      "text": "To start, we can use the method of comparison or interpolation to find the slope of the given line. \n\nStep 1: Rewrite the given equation in slope-intercept form:\n$2x + 3y = 6$\n\nStep 2: Solve for y:\n$3y = -2x + 6$\n$y = -\\frac{2}{3}x + 2$\n\nFrom the equation, we can see that the slope (m) of the given line is $-\\frac{2}{3}$.\n\nStep 3: Find the slope of the line that is parallel to the given line:\nSince parallel lines have the same slope, the slope of the line that is parallel to the given line is $-\\frac{2}{3}$.\n\nStep 4: Find the slope of the line that is perpendicular to the given line:\nTo find the slope of a line that is perpendicular to another line, we take the negative reciprocal of the slope of the given line. Therefore, the slope of the perpendicular line is $-\\frac{1}{-\\frac{2}{3}} = \\frac{3}{2}$.\n\nEquation:\n\nThe slope of the line that is perpendicular to the line $2x + 3y = 6$ is $\\boxed{\\frac{3}{2}}$.",
