    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "**Analysis:**\n\nTo find the slope of the line that is perpendicular to $2x + 3y = 6$, we first rewrite the given equation in slope-intercept form $y = mx + b$.\n\nStarting from the equation:\n\n\\[\n2x + 3y = 6\n\\]\n\nWe subtract $2x$ from both sides to isolate $3y$:\n\n\\[\n3y = -2x + 6\n\\]\n\nNext, we divide each term by 3 to solve for $y$:\n\n\\[\ny = \\frac{-2x}{3} + \\frac{6}{3}\n\\]\n\nThis equation is now in the slope-intercept form $y = mx + b$, where $m$ represents the slope. For the given equation $2x + 3y = 6$, the slope $m$ equals $-\\frac{2}{3}$. Since the slope of a perpendicular line is the negative reciprocal of the slope of the given line, the slope of the line perpendicular to the given line is $-\\frac{3}{2}$. Therefore, the slope of the line that is perpendicular to the line $2x + 3y = 6$ is $\\boxed{-\\frac{3}{2}}$.",
