    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find the slope of the line that is perpendicular to another line, we must first determine the slope of the original line.\n\nWe can rewrite $2x + 3y = 6$ in slope-intercept form, $y = mx + b$.\n\nSubtracting 2x from each side yields:\n\n\\[3y = -2x + 6\\]\n\nDividing each side by 3 implies:\n\n\\[\\frac{1}{3}y = -\\frac{2}{3}x + 2\\]\n\nRewriting this in slope-intercept form gives us:\n\n\\[y = -\\frac{2}{3}x + \\frac{6}{3}\\]\n\nThus, the slope of the original line is $-\\frac{2}{3}$.\n\nThe slope of a line perpendicular to another line is the negative reciprocal of the original line's slope. The negative reciprocal of a number can be found by multiplying it by -1 and then flipping the position of the fraction.\n\nSo we have:\n\n\\[-1 \\cdot -\\frac{2}{3} = \\frac{2}{3}\\]\n\\[\\frac{6}{3} \\div -1 \\cdot -\\frac{2}{3} = 6 \\div 3 \\cdot \\frac{2}{3} = 2 \\cdot \\frac{2}{3} = \\frac{4}{3}\\]\n\nTherefore, the slope of the line that is perpendicular to $2x + 3y = 6$ is $\\boxed{\\frac{4}{3}}$.",
